      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Express $3\\cos^2\\left(\\frac{16\\pi}{11}\\right)-\\frac{3}{2}$ as a single trigonometric ratio.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Collect the common factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&3\\cos^2\\left(\\frac{16\\pi}{11}\\right)-\\frac{3}{2}\\\\&=\\frac{3}{2}\\left(2\\cos^2\\left(\\frac{16\\pi}{11}\\right)-1\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the alternative cosine double-angle formula:\n\n$$\n2\\cos^2 x-1=\\cos(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=\\frac{16\\pi}{11}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos^2\\left(\\frac{16\\pi}{11}\\right)-1&=\\cos\\left(2\\cdot \\frac{16\\pi}{11}\\right)\\\\&=\\cos\\left(\\frac{32\\pi}{11}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKeep the outside factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{2}\\left(2\\cos^2\\left(\\frac{16\\pi}{11}\\right)-1\\right)&=\\frac{3}{2}\\cos\\left(\\frac{32\\pi}{11}\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
